<%#
  A real checkbox styled as a track + thumb, with `role="switch"` so assistive
  tech hears a switch, not a checkbox (GUIDELINES §3). The native input still
  drives the form: form.check_box emits the `writable=0` hidden field that
  flips the switch off, and the controller test still asserts on
  `[role=switch][checked]` / `[role=switch]:not([checked])`.

  The input is `appearance: none` and fills the track, so the global two-tone
  focus ring in application.css draws AROUND THE VISIBLE SWITCH when the
  control has keyboard focus — no per-component focus style, which rule 2.2
  bans. The track + thumb are drawn from `::before` on the input and shifted
  with the `peer-checked` modifier (the checked state is read off the input
  itself, so no second control is needed).

  Locals:
    form:      the form builder
    attribute: the attribute / param name
    label:     label text; defaults to the model's human attribute name
    input:     extra HTML attributes for the control (data-action, aria-*, …)
    class:     classes for the wrapper <label>

  No focus:outline-* / focus:ring-* here or in the styles below — the global
  ring is the only focus indicator (rule 2.2).
%>
<%
  input = local_assigns.fetch(:input, {}).dup
  label_text = local_assigns[:label]
  wrapper_class = local_assigns.fetch(:class, "")

  control_id = input[:id] || form.field_id(attribute)
  input[:id] = control_id
  input[:role] = "switch"
  input[:class] = class_names(
    "switch-input peer relative inline-block h-6 w-11 flex-none cursor-pointer appearance-none rounded-full border border-line bg-surface-hover transition-colors",
    "checked:bg-ink checked:border-ink",
    input[:class]
  )
%>
<label for="<%= control_id %>" class="flex cursor-pointer items-center gap-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink max-lg:min-h-11 <%= wrapper_class %>">
  <%= form.check_box attribute, input %>
  <%# The thumb is drawn from ::before on the input so it moves with the
      checked state, and stays out of the a11y tree (aria-hidden because the
      input itself is the one assistive tech focuses on). %>
  <span><%= label_text %></span>
</label>
